{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1143143-cross-contamination-chronicle-design-questions","id":1143143,"title":"Cross-Contamination (Chronicle Design Questions)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-09-22T21:40:25-07:00","id":1143143,"title":"Cross-Contamination (Chronicle Design Questions)","content":"I listen to a roleplaying podcast called <i>Friends at the Table</i>, whose current game was set up in a way that has inspired me to try to build a Requiem story to lean into the game's metaphysics about vampires tending to soak in their surroundings over time. Given the semi-experimental nature of this idea, I figured I should ask for some help in sorting out the fundamentals of how the game's framework is set up.<br /> <br /> To establish the basics: <i>Friends at the Table: The Twilight Mirage</i> began its post-initial-concept worldbuilding by having each of the group's players design the culture, philosophy, aesthetic, and extraordinary elements of the place their respective characters come from within the larger setting. The setting itself takes cues from each of those places to a lesser degree thanks to basic inter-party travel and communication, but the bloc as a whole maintains internal stability while the separate elements stay fairly distinct.<br /> <br /> The worldbuilding for these cultures is largely framed as answers to a series of questions, some of which are things like <i>What benefit does your party offer to the larger culture?</i> and <i>What other party is your party's favorite?</i> and <i>What did the death of your party's central figure take from the larger culture?</i> Some of these questions translate well to a vampiric domain with clans and covenants, but the way Requiem's world operates means a proper tailored list is essential to make this work right \u2014 a science fiction utopia in decline has different concerns than a horror setting. What sort of questions seem most pressing for determining how a clan or covenant inhabits the shared space of a domain and the larger organism of a city or ecosystem?<br /> <br /> In addition to the matter of how to phrase setting questions to best get the overall view of a given bloc, there's also the question of how much homebrew we want to build in \u2014 I'm designing this notionally as a sandbox game, but my group is fairly small and we're all still getting the hang of scheduling and pacing in relation to our day-to-day lives, so &quot;everyone design a clan and a covenant&quot; may be a bit much to expect from everyone, but the concept also loses something in the execution if we just plug in three or four corebook clans and covenants and derive a character spectrum identical to a typical game of Requiem. I'm leaning toward &quot;each player picks a canon clan or a canon covenant and designs the other character-axis themselves&quot; as a middle ground, but that could be suboptimal. I could also just have the group all pick or design a clan or covenant and even out the numbers myself (e.g. if we wind up with three clans and one covenant I would add another covenant). Does either of those seem more likely to work? Should I just take a page out of Masquerade's book and treat the clans of the domain as though they doubled as covenants?<br /> <br /> There's also the question of whether to make this a nomadic game or create a fixed domain that's also informed by the players' design choices (and, in either case, where in the world the game takes place). The aforementioned scheduling troubles mean it's entirely possible that I'll be one or two players short during a session and party division is often a fact of life, so knowing the tenor of the sandbox's emergent problems is important. A nomadic game runs into the problems of feeding and maintaining contact with Touchstones, but it allows a little more flexibility in terms of characters bringing in new resources and dividing their collective attention. On the other hand, a fixed domain offers more concrete stakes and the ability to develop one locale into something individual characters can be invested in. Either one has to handle the question of how large a group with multiple distinct clans and covenants is and how it's maintained that headcount across the centuries. Should I leave this up to player decision or just pick one outright? And which one seems more preferable in general?<br /> <br /> Finally, part of the idea as it exists in my head is that the setting design starts from the introduction of clans and covenants and then proceeds semi-randomly along the timeline to when the actual gameplay begins \u2014 events happen to the domain according to card draws and player response, which in turn shapes the history of the domain and the local culture of the clans and covenants as they exist when the PCs are Embraced. I don't want to spend too much time coming up with a prefabricated list of problems or figuring out what a more abstracted method translates to in context, but I'm quite sure I want a more organic method than me personally inventing problems and opportunities and throwing them at the map. Should I use a Tarot interpretation and work with each player to figure out what each draw's event signifies? Should I use a regular playing card deck and come up with a grid of thirteen events flavored by the suite of the card? Should I take another cue from <i>Friends at the Table</i> and run the worldbuilding as a set of <i><a href=\"https://buriedwithoutceremony.com/the-quiet-year/\" target=\"_blank\">The Quiet Year</a></i> or a similar small collaborative game? If I go with one of the first two options, when should I stop the drawing?<br /> <br /> In general, I'm trying to sort out a worldbuilding methodology for a game where the mutual influence of the Kindred on each other's development comes to the fore.<br /> <br /> Sorry that this is a lot; any help at all would be appreciated.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}